{"reference":"Jeremiah 15","book":"Jeremiah","chapter":15,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A First-Person Lament","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"In verses 10–21, the speaker moves from public judgment to a personal cry of distress: he describes conflict, isolation, reproach, joy in God’s words, and pain that seems unhealed. Passages like this are commonly called Jeremiah’s “confessions,” a label for first-person laments that can include accusation, protest, petition, compulsion, and despair. The label is useful for noticing the force of the passage, but its exact boundaries and what it reveals about biography remain disputed.","verse_refs":["Jeremiah 15:10-21"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_015_aa1695fd4a7cfe8b2a606c94"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}